> We utilize several error functions based on relevant economic relationships among the adaptation metrics:
> 
> 1. **Absolute Error (EA)**:
>    \[
>    E_A = \left| V_{obs} - V_{est} \right|
>    \]
>    where \(V_{obs}\) represents the observed value and \(V_{est}\) denotes the estimated value.
> 
> 2. **Squared Error (ES)**:
>    \[
>    E_S = \left( V_{obs} - V_{est} \right)^2
>    \]
>    This function is favored when emphasis is placed on larger discrepancies due to the squaring effect.
> 
> 3. **Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)**:
>    \[
>    RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left( V_{obs,i} - V_{est,i} \right)^2}
>    \]
>    where \(n\) is the total number of observations. RMSE provides a comprehensive view of the typical magnitude of the residuals.
> 
> #### 3. Optimization Problem
> 
> For finding optimal parameters, we define a multi-parameter optimization problem that aims to minimize the cumulative error across multiple relationships. The optimization function can be framed as:
